1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,915
A conveyor carries twenty parcels through
a bright warehouse.

2
00:00:03,915 --> 00:00:08,993
One fresh box slides into view, and a new
question interrupts the rhythm of

3
00:00:08,993 --> 00:00:09,356
production.

4
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:20,111
The warehouse bill includes rent, labour,
and a growing workload.

5
00:00:20,111 --> 00:00:25,910
Divide that whole bill among existing
parcels, and an average cost appears on

6
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:26,356
screen.

7
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:33,441
But the next parcel asks a different
question.

8
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:37,627
Some costs already exist, while extra
handling may become harder as the

9
00:00:37,627 --> 00:00:39,022
conveyor grows more crowded.

10
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:48,969
A smooth cost curve rises behind the
boxes.

11
00:00:48,969 --> 00:00:54,776
A straight line touches one point, showing
the nearby rate at which total cost

12
00:00:54,776 --> 00:00:55,606
is changing.

13
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:05,591
Move one parcel forward along the curve.
The tangent gives an estimate, while

14
00:01:05,591 --> 00:01:09,772
the curve gives an exact increment,
leaving a small visible gap.

15
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:20,307
The new parcel waits beside the receipt.
Can the average cost predict its

16
00:01:20,307 --> 00:01:24,606
extra cost, or should we read a different
number from the curve?
